::U1A01::Which statement best describes wellness? {=An active process involving multiple connected dimensions ~Only the absence of illness ~Athletic performance ~Physical appearance}

::U1A02::Which is a social-health example? {=Maintaining supportive relationships ~Sleeping eight hours ~Checking air quality ~Managing thoughts}

::U1A03::Poor sleep followed by low concentration and irritability illustrates what? {=Health dimensions can interact ~Appearance determines health ~Health is fixed ~One dimension never affects another}

::U1A04::Which is a protective factor? {=A trusted supportive adult ~Unsafe equipment ~Chronic sleep loss ~Ignoring warning signs}

::U1A05::Which conclusion is most defensible? {=A single health indicator cannot define total wellness ~Thinness proves wellness ~Athletic skill proves health ~A challenge means failure}

::U1A06::Which action shows personal responsibility without blaming someone for factors outside their control? {=Use available support and make a realistic controllable change ~Assume every condition is a personal failure ~Ignore environmental limits ~Avoid all assistance}

::U1A07::A student begins walking with a friend. Which two dimensions are most directly supported? {=Physical and social ~Environmental and financial ~Only emotional ~Only physical}

::U1A08::Which is an environmental-health influence? {=Unsafe air quality near an activity area ~Personal values ~A friendship ~A mood}

::U1A09::What is the first step in a systematic health decision? {=Clearly identify the decision or problem ~Choose immediately ~Ignore consequences ~Ask social media to decide}

::U1A10::Which is an internal influence? {=Personal values ~Advertising ~Family expectations ~Community resources}

::U1A11::Which is an external influence? {=Media messages ~Personal attitude ~Individual priorities ~Self-awareness}

::U1A12::A strong consequence analysis includes what? {=Short- and long-term effects on self and others ~Only immediate convenience ~Only positive outcomes ~A guess without evidence}

::U1A13::When is collaborative decision-making most appropriate? {=When a decision affects others or requires expertise ~Whenever avoiding responsibility is easier ~Only after acting ~Never}

::U1A14::Which source is most appropriate for a medical safety concern? {=A qualified health professional or responsible adult following procedure ~An anonymous rumor ~An advertisement alone ~A random comment}

::U1A15::A student lists options, predicts consequences, checks values and safety, then chooses. What process is being used? {=Systematic decision-making ~Impulse buying ~Goal avoidance ~Risk denial}

::U1A16::Which response best resists an unreliable media influence? {=Check evidence, purpose, and source credibility ~Accept repetition as proof ~Choose the most dramatic claim ~Ignore all evidence}

::U1A17::Which goal is SMART? {=For four weeks, I will walk 20 minutes on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday ~I will exercise more ~I will become perfectly healthy ~I will try harder someday}

::U1A18::What makes a goal measurable? {=It includes observable evidence or a count ~It sounds motivating ~It is difficult ~It uses broad language}

::U1A19::A baseline is what? {=Starting-point evidence used for comparison ~A final grade ~A punishment ~A medical diagnosis}

::U1A20::Which strategy best addresses forgetting activity shoes? {=Place them by the backpack the night before ~Make the goal less specific ~Ignore the barrier ~Increase intensity}

::U1A21::Which monitoring method best fits a goal of drinking water with lunch on five school days? {=A dated five-day checklist ~A general mood journal ~A monthly photograph ~A list of favorite drinks}

::U1A22::When should a goal be adjusted? {=When evidence shows the strategy is unsafe or not workable ~Whenever one feels impatient ~Never ~Only after success}

::U1A23::Which is a realistic modification for rain during a planned walk? {=Use an approved safe indoor low-impact option ~Walk outside despite hazardous weather ~Double intensity later ~Abandon all goals}

::U1A24::Why must monitoring match the goal? {=Evidence must measure the behavior or outcome named in the goal ~Any data prove progress ~Tracking is decorative ~Goals never require evidence}

::U1A25::What should happen before independent physical activity? {=Check readiness, environment, space, clothing, and equipment ~Begin at maximum effort ~Ignore weather ~Skip preparation}

::U1A26::Which sequence is safest? {=Warm-up, appropriate activity, cool-down ~Maximum effort, stop suddenly, stretch ~Activity, warm-up, cool-down ~Cool-down, activity, warm-up}

::U1A27::A student notices a serious warning sign during activity. What is the best response? {=Stop and seek appropriate assistance ~Push through pain ~Increase intensity ~Hide the symptom}

::U1A28::Modification means what? {=Changing an activity to reduce risk or improve appropriateness ~Failure ~Ignoring the goal ~Always doing more}

::U1A29::Which condition calls for postponing or moving activity? {=Lightning near an outdoor route ~A safe clear indoor space ~Comfortable weather ~Proper equipment}

::U1A30::Which statement about sweating is correct? {=Sweating alone does not prove workout quality ~More sweat always means more benefit ~Sweating eliminates all risk ~Sweating replaces hydration decisions}

::U1A31::Equipment is loose and cannot be secured. What decision is best? {=Stop or choose a safe alternative ~Continue carefully with broken equipment ~Increase speed ~Ignore it}

::U1A32::Why is cool-down included? {=It supports a gradual transition toward recovery ~It proves mastery ~It replaces preparation ~It permits unsafe intensity}

::U1A33::A complete plan begins with which paired information? {=A wellness focus and non-sensitive baseline ~A fitness prescription and diagnosis ~A reward and punishment ~Appearance and weight}

::U1A34::Which plan element addresses a schedule obstacle? {=A barrier-matched strategy ~A vague goal ~An unrelated monitoring tool ~Greater risk}

::U1A35::A goal targets three activity days weekly, but the student records only mood. What is the flaw? {=The monitoring does not measure activity frequency ~The goal is too safe ~The plan has a deadline ~Mood can never be recorded}

::U1A36::What completes the planning cycle after evaluation? {=A justified adjustment ~A diagnosis ~Maximum exertion ~Deletion of all evidence}

::U1A37::Which plan is strongest? {=A measurable goal with safe actions, matching evidence, barriers, support, and adjustment ~A broad wish with no evidence ~A difficult activity with no safety check ~A plan based on appearance}

::U1A38::Which is an appropriate support or resource? {=A trusted adult, approved reminder, or reliable service matched to the need ~An anonymous unsafe challenge ~A misleading advertisement ~Broken equipment}

::U1A39::A plan becomes unsafe in extreme heat. What is the best revision? {=Move to an approved safer environment or reschedule ~Ignore conditions ~Increase duration ~Remove hydration consideration}

::U1A40::Which evaluation rule is most useful? {=Compare weekly evidence with the measurable target and revise a barrier strategy if needed ~Judge success by appearance ~Assume effort proves results ~Never review the log}

